---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Intro from '../../components/case/Intro.astro';
import Meta from '../../components/case/Meta.astro';
import Stats from '../../components/case/Stats.astro';
import Chapter from '../../components/case/Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/case/Figure.astro';
import Lessons from '../../components/case/Lessons.astro';
import NextCase from '../../components/case/NextCase.astro';
import DiviWall from '../../components/case/DiviWall.astro';
import AiSlideshow from '../../components/case/AiSlideshow.astro';
import packWall from '../../assets/et/pack-wall.png';
import moduleFlow from '../../assets/et/module-flow.png';
import testLoop from '../../assets/et/test-loop.png';
---

<CaseLayout
  title="Designing Divi layouts for people who aren’t designers"
  description="Nine years at Elegant Themes, with 200+ Divi layout packs, a Figma source of truth for every Divi module, and the templates Divi AI builds pages with."
>
  <Intro
    title="Designing Divi layouts for people who aren’t designers"
    tags={['Elegant Themes', 'Divi + Divi AI', '2017 – 2026']}
    lede="For nine years I designed for Divi, a WordPress website builder used by nearly a million web designers and business owners. Most of them are not designers. The work moved in three steps, from the layouts people start from, to the Figma source of truth those layouts come from, to the templates Divi AI now builds pages with."
  >
    <DiviWall />
  </Intro>

  <Meta
    rows={[
      ['Role', 'Product Designer'],
      ['Company', 'Elegant Themes, remote with a US team'],
      ['Product', 'Divi visual builder and Divi AI'],
      ['Period', 'Aug 2017 – Sep 2026'],
    ]}
  />

  <aside class="note">
    <p class="note__title">What I can show</p>
    <p class="note__text">
      Elegant Themes keeps its internal files and resources private. Everything on this page is my own design work.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <Stats
    items={[
      ['200+', 'layout packs I designed and shipped'],
      ['300+', 'section templates Divi AI builds from'],
      ['12', 'niches covered, with 40+ categories inside them'],
    ]}
  />

  <Chapter title="Layouts for people who aren’t designers">
    <p class="p">
      Most of my time at Elegant Themes went into layout packs. A layout pack is a full set of pages for one kind of business that anyone
      can import into Divi and make their own. I designed more than 200 of them, plus campaign templates for Black
      Friday and other promotions.
    </p>
    <p class="p">
      The people using them are small business owners and freelancers. They replace every photo and most of the copy, so
      a pack is a starting point, not a finished site. I designed for the version they would end up with, not the one in
      the preview.
    </p>
    <Figure
      src={packWall}
      alt="Twelve of my Divi layout packs, among them a barber shop, an event venue, a bagel shop, an online store, a bike repair shop and a meditation centre. The wall fades out under a pill that reads “Just a handful of them”."
    />
    <p class="p">Two rules held for every pack, whoever ended up editing it.</p>
    <div class="rules">
      <div class="rule">
        <p class="rule__title">Readable on any phone</p>
        <p class="rule__text">Body text never drops below a set minimum size on mobile.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <p class="rule__title">Contrast checked</p>
        <p class="rule__text">Every layout runs through a contrast check before it ships.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter title="One Figma source of truth for every Divi module">
    <p class="p">
      Divi’s modules were designed by another team. My job was to rebuild every existing module, and each new one as it
      came, as a Figma component that matches what the builder can actually produce. Sections, templates and full layouts
      are then assembled from those components instead of drawn from scratch.
    </p>
    <Figure src={moduleFlow} alt="Flow from Divi module to Figma component to section to layout pack." />
    <p class="p">
      Matching meant the component carries the same settings as the module, so a designer can’t make something a Divi
      user can’t rebuild.
    </p>
    <p class="p">I also wrote the Divi Build Guidelines, which became part of the internal design system.</p>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter title="Getting the templates ready for Divi AI">
    <p class="p">
      When Elegant Themes built Divi AI, the section templates it generates pages from came out of this library. I
      structured them so they could be built back into Divi and extended to new topics without breaking what already
      worked.
    </p>
    <p class="p">I didn’t build the AI or design its interface. I built what it builds from.</p>
    <p class="p">
      My part of the testing was simple to describe and slow to do. I gave the AI real requests and checked whether it
      understood the components and used them the way they were meant to be used.
    </p>
    <Figure
      src={testLoop}
      alt="The test loop. Request, generated page, right components? If not, either fix my side (templates and components) or pass to engineering (prompt and model)."
    />
    <AiSlideshow />
  </Chapter>

  <Lessons
    title="What I carried forward"
    items={[
      ['Design for the person who edits it last.', 'A layout is judged after a non-designer has changed it, not in the preview.'],
      ['A design only counts if the product can build it.', 'Matching Figma to the builder removed a whole class of “looks right, can’t be made” work.'],
      ['Generated output is only as good as the system under it.', 'The quality of Divi AI’s pages depends on the templates and components it draws from.'],
    ]}
  />

  <NextCase current="elegant-themes" />
</CaseLayout>

<style>
  .note {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 6px;
    padding: 18px 20px;
    border-radius: 12px;
    background: var(--bg);
  }
  .note__title {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 20px;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .note__text {
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 24px;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }
  .rules {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 12px;
  }
  .rule {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 6px;
    padding: 16px;
    border-radius: 12px;
    background: var(--bg);
  }
  .rule__title {
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 22px;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .rule__text {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 20px;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .rules {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
